The '''Burawa Open''' ({{lang|so|Furitaanka Burawa}}) is an annual international tennis tournament held in [[Samara]], [[Burawa]], at the National Tennis Center. Founded in 1893 during the early republic period, it is one of the country’s major sporting events and is commonly known as the Samara Open. The tournament is held each year in the first half of July, lasts 14 days, and serves as a warm-up event before the Grand Slam season. It is affiliated with the Adonian World Tennis Organization (AWTO), although it is not part of any official circuit. The tournament carries a prize pool of $10,000,000 and does not accept commercial sponsorships; it is funded by the Government of Burawa through the Ministry of Humanity, together with ticket sales, merchandise, and international broadcasting revenue.

The Burawa Open accepts entrants from all nationalities through open preliminary rounds. Players who meet the annual minimum AWTO ranking may enter the main draw directly, while lower-ranked entrants must advance through qualification. The tournament includes men’s, women’s, and mixed events in both singles and doubles.

== History ==

The Burawa Open was established in 1893, when tennis was becoming an organised national sport in the early republic. Samara was selected as the host city because it was the capital and already had the National Tennis Center and related sporting infrastructure required for a major hard-court tournament.

From 1911 to 1930, the tournament formed part of the Gentlemen's League, a tennis series contested by Volisania, Aetolia, and Mercia. As Volisanian-Burawi tensions increased, Burawa withdrew from the arrangement. The tournament was suspended during the [[Burawi Domestic War]] (1932–1935) and the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1948), missing 17 consecutive editions. After the war, the Burawa Open operated independently and came under direct funding from the Ministry of Humanity.

In its early decades, the tournament grew into one of the most visible annual fixtures in Burawi sport. Its July timing gradually gave it a distinctive role in the international calendar, since players could use it as preparation for the Grand Slam season that followed a month later. The event’s prominence was reinforced by its AWTO affiliation and by the fact that it drew both domestic and foreign competitors.

== Format ==

The Burawa Open is played on hard courts. The competition is organised around separate men’s and women’s draws, together with mixed events. Open preliminary rounds are available to all entrants, but the main draw is reserved for players who satisfy the minimum AWTO ranking threshold for that year.

The event is structured to accommodate both established professionals and lower-ranked challengers. As a result, it combines the status of a major open tournament with the accessibility of a broad qualifying system. This format has helped make the tournament a recurring opportunity for emerging players to face higher-ranked opposition before the Grand Slam season.

== Venue ==

The tournament is held at the National Tennis Center in Samara. The venue is configured around hard courts and is regarded as the principal tennis facility in Burawa. Its location in the capital has made it convenient for national media coverage, diplomatic attendance, and visiting international players.

== Administration ==

The Burawa Open is fully funded by the Government of Burawa. Revenue from ticket sales, merchandise, and international broadcasting coverage supports the tournament, and commercial sponsorships are not accepted.

== Traditions ==

A local music week accompanies the Burawa Open each year. Concerts, food stalls, and related cultural programming are organised around the tournament, giving the event a broader civic atmosphere beyond the tennis competition itself. The tradition has become closely associated with the tournament’s identity in Samara.

The Open Shield is awarded to the champions of the tournament. In Burawi sporting culture, the trophy is treated as one of the more prestigious prizes in the annual tennis calendar.
